; metacalc formula drawing for the TI-84 Plus SE, first slice. Lays out one statement of the
; library, unfolded, and draws it on the canvas pixel for pixel as the host model does
; (crates/mcui, crates/mcglyph): the tokens of the statement, each the glyph codes of its symbol
; from the library's glyph map, placed in lines by mcglyph's place, drawn with ui_text at x plus
; the token's column on the text row of its line, only on rows inside a window. Folding, the
; ellipsis, the cursor and parse trees are not here.
;
; Code runs from flash in bank A and is position dependent. An including file defines FM_EMBEDDED
; first and places the code itself, after the reader (lib/) and the canvas (ui/) it calls, with -I
; at the repository root and at app/src. The canvas's ui_set_font must have been called before
; fm_statement measures a token and before fm_draw draws one.
;
; Memory, placed by app/src/ram_map.inc. FM_RAM is in bank C of the kernel's spare page
; KERNEL_RAM_PAGE, from KERNEL_RAM_HIGH, $1600 bytes, which the kernel uses only inside a kernel
; call; fm maps the page in every public routine and keeps everything there:
;   +$000 $100  variables (fm/ram.inc)
;   +$100 $100  fm's private stack, FM_STACK_TOP at +$200
;   +$200 $800  FM_GLYPHS, the library's glyph map section as lib_glyphs finds it
;   +$A00 $800  FM_SECOND, room for a second section of at most 2,048 bytes, unused
;   +$1200 $100 symbol ids of the loaded statement, at most 255
;   +$1300 $100 token widths in pixels
;   +$1400 $100 the columns between a token and the one before it, 1 or 3
;   +$1500 $100 token spacing flags
; FM_SCRATCH is 256 bytes in bank B, in saveSScreen after UI_VARS, for the buffers the reader's
; accessors write; it holds nothing between calls.
;
; Sessions. Every public routine runs as a session, as the kernel's calls do: it reads the
; interrupt state with ld a,i twice, turns interrupts off, saves port 5 and SP, maps the spare page,
; switches to FM_STACK and works there, and on every exit restores port 5, SP and the interrupt
; state. The reader and the canvas run inside it: lib_read saves the caller's port 5, which is the
; spare page, and puts it back, with no stack instruction in between, and needs its buffer in bank B;
; the canvas touches only bank B, the stack and the font table it was given. A session calls no OS
; routine. Port 7 is never changed by fm.
;
; The kernel's contract. On entry a session reads the kernel's ready mark, kv_ready. A mark that is
; set means kernel_init has run since the last session and may have written the high region, so
; fm is closed: every routine but fm_open then returns FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN and draws nothing. Then the
; session always clears the mark, so kernel_verify refuses until kernel_init runs again. A caller
; opens fm again with fm_open, which copies the glyph section afresh. The memory of an app that
; ran before holds a stale open mark until fm_open or a session sees a set mark, so an app calls
; fm_open once the library is open.
;
; Calling. Status comes back in A, 0 meaning ok, with the zero flag set exactly when A is 0. Every
; routine destroys all registers but IY and keeps the reader's rule for ports 5 and 7 and for the
; interrupt state, which are the caller's again on every exit. A session pushes at most 14 bytes on
; its private stack, the call into the reader or the canvas included.
;
;   fm_open       in:  the reader open with lib_open
;                 out: A = status. Copies the whole glyph section into FM_GLYPHS, checks that
;                      every entry lies inside it, and marks fm open. Closed until it returns 0.
;                      FM_ERR_CLOSED: the reader is not open. FM_ERR_NO_MAP: the library has no
;                      glyph map. FM_ERR_FONT: its map is for another font. FM_ERR_TOO_BIG: the
;                      section is longer than 2,048 bytes. FM_ERR_BAD: an entry the section
;                      cannot hold. FM_ERR_READ: the reader refused a read.
;   fm_statement  in:  HL = assertion number, E = statement, numbered as lib_tree numbers them:
;                      the essential hypotheses in record order from 0, FFh the conclusion
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of tokens. The tokens are the statement's
;                      symbols as lib_hypothesis and lib_conclusion give them, the typecode
;                      first. FM_ERR_INDEX: no such assertion or statement. FM_ERR_BAD: a
;                      symbol past the glyph map, or a token wider than 255 pixels. A refused
;                      call leaves no statement loaded.
;   fm_rows       in:  D = width in pixels, 0 to 255
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of lines the loaded statement takes. A token
;                      wider than the width sits alone on its line.
;   fm_draw       in:  HL = text row of the statement's first line, signed, -16384 to 16383;
;                      D = width in pixels; E = x of the statement's first column; B = the first
;                      text row of the window, C = one past its last
;                 out: A = status. Draws each line whose row is in the window, with ui_text,
;                      and nothing else.
; Other statuses: FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN, from fm_statement, fm_rows and fm_draw.

#ifndef FM_EMBEDDED
.org $4000
#endif

#include "fm/ram.inc"
#include "fm/session.inc"
#include "fm/open.inc"
#include "fm/statement.inc"
#include "fm/layout.inc"
